@@ -0,0 +1,176 @@
+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Ordinary NODE_HANG handler retirement
+====================================
+
+The process-private retirement path supports the ordinary NODE_HANG producer
+only. It requires the actual BQL-held, paused, drained single-threaded RR stop.
+Other producer families retain their permanent registry and impulse edges and
+return ENOTSUP. Selected construction continues to refuse before allocation
+without a genuine Source payer; none of these journals supplies that payer or
+changes activation.
+
+The actual node constructor creates the registration and its canonical holder
+inside the combined handler allocation. Submit and aggregate restore retain
+that same registration through validation and pending-command ownership.
+Staging, copied/restored rules, event reservations and the actual lifecycle hang
+state acquire separate intrusive children before their ordinary allocations.
+Transfer and queue unlink preserve membership. The real destructors record
+allocation, payload and callback returns separately; an empty queue or returned
+reference does not consume those children.
+
+Rule visitors retain both an actual rule reference and a separate entered
+reader child. The real visitor return and its independent post are recorded
+before that child can close. The existing recursive storage operation serializes
+ordinary reference changes, destruction and receipt observation. Retirement
+uses trylock under the fault registry mutex, so a concurrent reader refuses
+retirement instead of creating an inverted wait. An actual aggregate callback
+journal authenticates restore construction. The actual registry removal
+interval acquires an exclusion against new aggregate callback entry before
+checking children, and retains it through unpublication and destruction.
+A refused prebirth retirement releases its exclusion. An uncertain terminal
+post keeps the external cursor and its remaining exclusions.
+
+QObject body ownership
+----------------------
+
+Returning a rule's JSON reference is distinct from returning the shared JSON
+body. Actual NODE_HANG parsing enters an ordinary construction journal with a
+canonical field retained by that registration. The real QBool, QNum, QDict,
+QList and QString constructors allocate their own typed bodies and container
+entries through the journal's allocation producer. Dictionary keys and retained
+strings are produced there too. A retained GString is copied into a separately
+owned exact string allocation; its original parser scratch is released through
+its ordinary GLib path. No interface adopts a caller-provided allocation.
+QObject layouts are unchanged.
+
+Each enrollment control is allocated before its payload. Checked byte totals
+record the requested typed payload extents and the journal/enrollment control
+extents. Issuance does not wrap. Overflow retains an error instead of permitting
+clean retirement of untracked output. Actual typed destructors record a body
+receipt only after their own g_free returns. A shared nested QObject therefore
+keeps its construction journal live after the rule and JSON root references
+return. A QObject refcount, a root unref and registry absence cannot substitute
+for those destructor receipts. Parser nonreturn or an unresolved allocation
+keeps the registration retained.
+
+These are ordinary allocated journals. Their own controls remain allocated
+until all their actual body receipts exist, and their external returning cursor
+is populated before control destruction. The fixed process-private registries
+are included in the native fixed-storage inventory getter. The dynamic totals
+describe requested allocations and actual allocator calls, not GLib allocator
+metadata, arena backing, parser scratch funding or a selected allocation grant.
+No process/image backing is refunded.
+
+Terminal ordering and evidence
+------------------------------
+
+The terminal cut first authenticates the real fault registry removal interval,
+excludes restore entry, and checks every retained pending, validation, storage,
+reader and JSON child. It then removes the exact handler under the fault mutex
+and verifies actual absence independently. Only then does it unpublish the exact
+impulse slot and destroy the retained child controls, graph controls and combined
+producer allocation. NODE_HANG retirement is permanent for that process; a new
+registration cannot reuse its storage or reopen its impulse edge.
+
+A separate static returning cursor survives the producer free. It retains the
+original canonical holder coordinate without reading that interior field after
+free, along with independent first/post and physical-return facts. Failed post
+retains this cursor. Retrying cannot repeat a completed destructor; copied or
+stale holders cannot issue a new retirement. Successful handler retirement does
+not retire global family callbacks, lifecycle arrays, CPUs, the fault registry
+container, allocator backing or Source.
+
+The ordinary initialized fixture accepts ``--retire-registration`` together
+with ``--aggregate --prime-genesis``. It uses actual normal installation,
+registration, duplicate refusal, framed Submit, PREPARE_ONLY, commit/free-state,
+retained NODE_HANG rules, unconsumed ARM events and complete public aggregate
+save/load. It checks live restore intervals, a real second-thread rule reader
+and a retained nested JSON string before terminal success. Causal controls
+independently rebuild actual producer/destructor/reader/unpublication bodies;
+no populated owner or accepting Source provider is supplied by the fixture.
+The existing one-MiB machine configuration and thirty-second execution limit
+remain unchanged.
+
+This fixture establishes ordinary component execution. Selected allocation,
+installed module callback cleanup, other fault families, deferred operations,
+CPU/process/image retirement and the mandatory launched Source/Root lifecycle
+remain separate, unqualified paths. Ordinary generic container reference-return
+facts outside the supported NODE_HANG graph do not imply complete GLib body
+or allocator-backing destruction.
+
+Consumed-negative JSON journal return
+-------------------------------------
+
+A QObject journal can destroy its enrollment controls and journal before its
+independent return post refuses. The enclosing NODE_HANG JSON owner records the
+actual physical receipt separately from that negative status. It retains the
+original cause, its consumed canonical field and the receipt on every later
+terminal attempt; it does not call the destroyed journal again. A zero status
+without the physical receipt also refuses outer destruction. A consumed field
+without that receipt retains uncertainty rather than proving physical return.
+
+The ``json-post`` fixture control injects EIO at the real return post after a
+nonempty journal's actual enrollment and journal frees. The real parsed graph
+has already passed its typed destructors. It checks the surviving receipt,
+retained handler and outer JSON owner, unchanged retry receipt and refusal of
+new journal construction. Independently rebuilt ``json-post-return`` and
+``json-post-retention`` controls omit the physical fact and saved outer cause.
+The ``json-journal-return`` control omits the physical fact on the otherwise
+successful return path. The ordinary success and unconsumed body-destructor
+refusal remain separate controls. These fault injections grant no Source
+funding, selected admission, allocator-backing release or launched authority.
+
+Ordinary NODE_HANG envelope storage
+----------------------------------
+
+The immutable admitted rule determines the actual event plan. Activation and
+recovery each use 192 bytes of evidence; an enabled watchdog additionally
+reserves the maximum of its 192-byte composition and 304-byte winning lifecycle
+transcripts. Each body includes the complete 192-byte envelope header and exact
+request bytes. Checked capacities precede body allocation, reservation splitting
+and commit. Capacity does not change the eventual encoded length: the ordinary
+disabled-watchdog fixture still publishes the complete 630-byte ARM envelope.
+
+A separate retained registration child precedes each flexible body allocation.
+It records the requested typed extent, actual immutable request hash/sequence,
+role and current process-private custodian. Splitting and emission transfer that
+same body; they do not allocate a replacement. Emission validates its real rule
+origin and complete body capacity before consuming a row credit. Row credits do
+not fund bytes. This is ordinary GLib allocation, with no claim about allocator
+arena/backing release or selected byte admission.
+
+Aggregate restore authenticates its actual entered registration owner, creates
+a separately owned temporary event row and allocates the accepted complete
+body before copying validated bytes. Commit transfers the same body to its
+actual static queue row. The temporary row, replaced body, unused reserved
+bodies and polled body each have distinct physical returns. Storage operations
+serialize transfers, consumers and destructors; the retained child survives
+both its body and container. Returning revokes readers before free. A failed
+post retains its original cause and custodian, blocks further body access and
+prevents a repeated free or registration retirement.
+
+The dedicated emergency row remains available to the actual internal-terminal
+producer naming a retained NODE_HANG rule. Its exact 128-byte resource transcript
+uses a separate late ordinary allocation authenticated by that rule's real
+registration origin. It is not part of the earlier two/three-row reservation
+plan and has no selected payer. The emergency fixture ends with genuine pending,
+rule, CPU and process dependencies still outstanding after observing body return;
+process exit does not return them.
+
+The initialized ordinary fixture exercises full aggregate save/load, unchanged
+ARM bytes, public polling, REMOVE, natural drainage and supported registration
+retirement. Independent omissions cover body birth, transfer, requested-payload
+and physical receipts, restore-row return and saved failed-post facts. The
+emergency failed-post control performs the actual body free, checks the surviving
+physical receipt and retries the public consumer without another free.
+
+The enabled-watchdog fixture captures genuine 304-byte evidence at the actual
+terminal staging boundary and checks its preborn body's geometry. Its independent
+capacity omission fails there. The subsequent actual authorized-boundary stop
+admission still refuses in this ordinary component; that refusal is preserved.
+Enabled-watchdog queue delivery, deferred/terminal return, other-family body
+ownership, selected allocation and the launched Source/Root lifecycle remain
+unqualified. The direct unowned codec fixture establishes only factual wire
+validation. No copied stream, caller pointer, scalar status or NULL holder issues
+a registration child, Source authority or physical receipt.
