+.. SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later
+
+Publisher subscription and command lender proposal
+==================================================
+
+Status and existing boundary
+----------------------------
+
+This is a paired protocol proposal, not an implemented capability or issuer.
+The QEMU checkout contains the receiver and its process-private Source owners;
+the external publisher and installed transport module are absent. Their real
+membership constructors/destructors must join this receiver before availability.
+Quantum acquisition, observation, progress and return are shared-memory-only.
+Socket traffic is confined to startup lender/capability establishment.
+
+The existing selected route consumes Setup 5, tag 0x15, on its retained plain UNIX
+stream endpoint. Its five/six-descriptor roster includes the standing publication
+and cancellation roles. Source's later Hello sends 13 bytes, tag 0xf0, transport 8
+and shared-memory ABI 33; the 21-byte tag 0xf1 reply gives node index/count. Those
+fixed frames have no subscription capability. Existing editions remain exact.
+
+The four ``qemu_crucible_source_publisher_action_*_v1`` methods have declarations
+and callers but no bodies; ``QemuCruciblePublisherActionLoan`` is incomplete.
+Member edition 5 retains the actual module child, Bridge, runtime, setup VMA and
+slot, and binds reader/Park/payload/sample-action callbacks. It binds no producer
+subscription. Command receive and Main payload custody cannot issue one.
+
+Two existing regions have different encodings. ABI 33 node-slot fields use
+little-endian atomics, including advance sequence at 208, action hold at 256 and
+192 action-return bytes at 264. Their generated ABI header is an external build
+input, not maintained here. The distinct 1504-byte standing lender uses
+big-endian atomic 64-bit words, with a 1496-byte payload. Both are observations;
+neither implements physical publisher memberships. This proposal repurposes
+neither region, nor any padding or reserved field.
+
+Startup establishment only
+--------------------------
+
+Proposed Setup 6 uses tag 0x16 and exactly 120 payload bytes: the existing 56-byte
+Setup 5 payload followed by BEu64 lender generation at 56, requested region bytes
+at 64, device at 72, inode at 80, producer-to-receiver event token at 88 and
+receiver-to-producer event token at 96; BEu32 lender schema 1 at 104 and flags 1
+at 108; reserved zero 112..119. Message length is 121 and total frame 125.
+The SCM roster is the existing five/six roles followed by the command region,
+producer-to-receiver doorbell and receiver-to-producer doorbell: eight/nine
+roles total. There is no late receive, optional fallback or unpriced alias.
+
+The sealed purpose plan needs a new matched edition that binds these actual
+roles, source/process/account identity, lender generation, concrete requested
+and backing extents, kernel event roles and the enlarged fixed Source floor.
+The external producer must construct those physical owners and retain the same
+child/family/endpoint before sending them. Their bytes, FD numbers, inode
+identity and available capacity do not construct the ownership. The precise
+purpose-plan extension must be implemented with that real producer, not filled
+from this receiver's observations.
+
+Proposed Hello transport 9 advertises subscription capability bit 0. Its 21-byte
+frame is BEu32 length 17, tag 0xf0, transport 9 at 5, ABI 33 at 9 and BEu64 requested
+capabilities at 13. Reply 37 is length 33, tag 0xf1, transport 9 at 5, ABI 33 at 9, node
+index at 13, node count at 17, accepted capability mask at 21 and BEu64 lender
+generation at 29. Unknown bits refuse. Generation must match the already retained
+actual Setup 6 lender. The bit describes support, not permission or payment.
+No capability may be advertised until both real producer and receiver owners
+exist. Existing Hello 8/Setup 5 users retain their exact behavior; there is no
+selected subscription fallback to them.
+
+The real module remains the sole stream/parser owner. Source must not introduce
+a competing FD3 receiver. A matched member edition must bind that same actual
+child's command-region lender and doorbells to its existing Bridge/runtime/slot,
+retaining their real physical references before any result. Registration alone
+issues no semantic Original. There are no per-command socket messages, including
+no socket Acquire, Current, Return or return acknowledgement.
+
+Dedicated shared region
+-----------------------
+
+The producer creates an actual O_RDWR memfd, fixes its length to 4096, and
+adds F_SEAL_SHRINK, F_SEAL_GROW and F_SEAL_SEAL before lending it. F_SEAL_WRITE
+and F_SEAL_FUTURE_WRITE are incompatible with the required mutable mappings;
+unknown seals refuse in this edition. Both real aliases retain FD_CLOEXEC.
+The constructor verifies the actual kernel seals/length/roles under the same
+retained issuer, then performs MAP_SHARED with PROT_READ|PROT_WRITE and requires
+actual MADV_DONTFORK success. Equal fstat facts alone issue no lender.
+
+The requested region is 4096 bytes, schema 1. Obtain the actual page size from
+the retained runtime/kernel page-size observation, require a nonzero power of
+two, and check ``4096 + page_size - 1`` before rounding down by its alignment
+mask. Charge that actual rounded backing, both real mapping controls, descriptor
+aliases, eventfd kernel objects and fixed owner journals before their births.
+Do not assume host pages are 4096 bytes. Mutable file and mapped-memory roles
+must join their existing distinct accounts without inventing or duplicating a
+payer. Mapping/seal/alias failures retain their real acquisition journals.
+It is one region for this real Source incarnation, with one outstanding command/loan. All public words in this
+new region are aligned big-endian atomic 64-bit words. No native C layout, pointer,
+function, mutex or enum is shared. The receiver has its own process-private
+canonical journal. The actual target must support lock-free process-shared 64-bit
+atomics; a library lock fallback cannot implement this protocol.
+
+Header protocol flags are reserved zero in schema 1. The immutable 256-byte
+header contains magic ``CRUCPLM1`` at 0; BEu32 schema 1 at 8
+and requested bytes 4096 at 12; lender generation at 16; protocol flags at 24;
+account/process generations at 32/40; source capability 32 bytes at 48 and source
+reservation 32 bytes at 80; node index/count as BEu32 at 112/116; BEu32 publication
+offset/bytes 256/1536 at 120/124, receiver offset/bytes 1792/512 at 128/132 and
+terminal offset/bytes 2304/512 at 136/140; zero 144..255. Bytes 2816..4095 are zero.
+These fixed disjoint spans require exact bounds checks before any output or
+state mutation. Header identity must join the actual retained Source/slot/lender
+receipts, not an arbitrary supplied mapping. No cross-region reference exists.
+
+All accesses to mutable spans use atomic 64-bit words, including snapshots, so
+concurrent readers have no C data race. Initial implementation should use the
+same conservative sequentially consistent word operations as the existing
+standing snapshot. Producer writes odd sequence before changing publication,
+then the complete atomic-word payload, then releases the next even sequence.
+Receiver takes exactly one bounded before/data/after observation and requires
+identical nonzero even sequence. Zero, odd, busy or changed sequence gives a
+typed unavailable/busy/changed refusal; there is no in-function retry loop.
+Deadline-bounded reentry is a separate operation under the same retained owner,
+never a fresh loan. Snapshot scratch can be tentative, but no decoded output or
+semantic use is published on refusal. Exhaustion refuses before wrap.
+Optimization requires a separate ordering proof and measurements; it must not weaken this contract.
+
+Publication span
+----------------
+
+Publication offset 256 has the following 1536-byte canonical layout. Offset
+numbers in this table are relative to that span.
+
+============ ====== ==========================================================
+Offset       Bytes  Meaning
+============ ====== ==========================================================
+0            8      Publication sequence, producer-written odd/even
+8            8      Actual producer offer/loan generation
+16           8      Issued ticket serial, nonwrapping and at most 2^61-1
+24           8      Producer permission phase: Borrowable 1, Revoked 2, Terminal 3
+32           8      Standing publication sequence bound to this cohort
+40           8      Command-present flag 0/1
+48           8      Cohort offset 128 and length 896, BEu32 each
+56           8      Command offset 1024 and length 128/0, BEu32 each
+64           64     Reserved zero
+128          896    Complete existing ``CRUCACT2`` schema 2 cohort
+1024         128    Immutable command below; bytes 80..127 always zero
+1152         384    Reserved zero
+============ ====== ==========================================================
+
+The immutable cohort retains all existing source capability/reservation,
+account/process incarnation, request/capture/frontier and publisher-birth
+coordinates. Row positions remain distinct memberships even if lender/operation
+bytes match. Born and projected masks are separate; an unprojected born row
+still carries physical debt. The actual producer retains each row membership,
+immutable command and recipient-specific ticket before publishing Borrowable.
+The permission word can revoke semantic use while leaving that physical loan
+retained. Its transitions are monotonic Borrowable to Revoked to Terminal;
+permission cannot be restored under the same command publication. Apart from
+this permission word, the held command publication cannot be changed or reused until both terminal consumption and receiver closure.
+
+An immutable command's first 80 bytes are magic ``CRUCQCM1`` at 0, BEu32 schema 1
+at 8 and bytes 128 at 12, then BEu64 scheduler generation at 16, initial raw icount
+at 24, max-advance raw icount at 32, effective absolute logical-tick ceiling at 40,
+effective native monotonic-nanosecond ceiling at 48, BEu32 stop mode at 56 and
+reserved zero at 60, BEu64 command frontier at 64 and next-idle target generation
+at 72. Stop 1 means retired-ceiling completion; stop 2 permits this command's
+authenticated next-idle completion. The scheduler counterpart must define the
+exact conversion from its actual variants; the existing slot byte is not cast
+into this enumeration.
+
+Scheduler generation is the genuine nonzero even nonwrapping scheduler
+publication, distinct from standing sequence, lender/offer generation and ticket
+serial. Effective ceilings come from its actual paid owner. Preparation, reset
+and external restore cannot be relabeled Quantum. Its class 1 primary Original
+must independently exist in the same retained cohort. Non-Quantum commands have
+command-present 0 and all 128 command bytes zero; the cohort is not retagged.
+
+
+Canonical validation constraints
+--------------------------------
+
+The following are pure byte-syntax constraints, not ownership predicates. A
+``generation`` in this section means an integer in 1..UINT64_MAX-1. A
+``sequence`` means a nonzero even integer in 2..UINT64_MAX-1. Neither definition
+permits wrapping; bytes alone cannot prove that a real producer never wrapped.
+A ticket serial is in 1..2^61-1. Ticket phases are exactly 1..6; phases 0 and 7
+refuse, and the transition table below separately restricts their writers.
+There is no ticket decoder or atomic transition implementation in this cut.
+
+Every span has its exact stated byte length and magic/schema/length when those
+fields exist. All reserved bytes are zero. All multibyte integers are big endian;
+BEi32 statuses use two's-complement encoding. Header lender, account and process
+identities are generations; capability and reservation are each nonzero 32-byte
+strings. Node count is 1..32 and index is strictly less than count. Header flags
+are zero and all three offset/length pairs must equal the canonical geometry,
+not merely fit within the region. Header decoding validates only bytes 0..255;
+the future full-region constructor must also validate zero bytes 2816..4095.
+
+Publication sequence and standing sequence are sequences; loan identity is a
+generation; ticket serial has the bound above. Permission phase is exactly
+1..3, command-present is exactly 0 or 1, and both cohort/command offset/length
+pairs are canonical even when the command is absent. The cohort must be present,
+and its publisher-birth sequence must not exceed the standing sequence.
+The complete existing CRUCACT2 schema 2 constraints remain in force, including
+these rules:
+
+* Its source capability/reservation are nonzero; account/process incarnations
+  are nonzero (their existing syntax permits UINT64_MAX). Request token is even,
+  including zero; capture generation is zero or odd, including UINT32_MAX.
+  Publisher birth is a sequence; command frontier permits every u64 value.
+* State uses only mask 0x800f0f03: primary count 1 or 2, born bits 8..11,
+  projected bits 16..19 and dirty bit 31. Planned count is between primary count
+  and 4. Born mask is nonzero and names only planned rows; projected is a subset
+  of born. A clean record requires both masks equal every planned row; partial
+  masks require dirty. Dirty preserves incomplete physical-debt metadata and
+  grants no currentness. The same clean/full rule applies to CRUCPRK1 Park.
+* Each unprojected or unplanned 192-byte row is entirely zero. A projected row
+  has nonzero lender capability and operation, producing class 0..13, state
+  0..3 and poll interval 1..INT64_MAX. Budget-owner zero denotes the root budget
+  owner; all u64 budget-owner values and nonzero u64 operation values are valid.
+  Original start is at least origin, genuine-progress time is at least start,
+  required-work inventory is positive, and completed required units do not
+  exceed required units. No other ordering or zero/MAX restriction is added
+  to its u64 revisions, times or unit counters.
+* Row bytes 168..191 are zero. Primary rows have role 0. Planned preparation rows
+  have role 1, class 12 and no preparation-row link. A nonzero primary link is
+  greater than primary count and at most planned count. A projected nonclass 1
+  row has at least one nonzero total/progress/outer/private-interval end time.
+  Row positions and all four born debts remain distinct.
+
+The public process contract preserves root-budget-owner zero while requiring
+positive required-work inventory for every projected row. Neither scalar issues
+ownership or currentness. Earlier inherited decoder behavior incorrectly
+rejected root-owner zero and allowed zero inventory; the separate compatibility
+checkpoint corrects those two predicates without changing row layout or the
+other constraints. The existing sample ActionOriginal admission additionally
+requires exactly one primary required unit; positive inventory alone admits no
+operation.
+
+
+Park's distinct CRUCPRK1 binding requires request correlation in
+1..UINT64_MAX-1, not an Action request-token/capture pair. Each projected
+primary row is class 6 (quiescence). When both primaries are projected, their
+(lender capability, budget owner, operation) identities differ in at least one
+component. Root budget-owner zero remains valid; operation remains nonzero.
+Dirty missing projections stay zero and retain all born debt, without inventing
+a second primary identity or admitting a ParkOriginal. These are additional
+Park byte-syntax checks, not physical ownership or completion evidence.
+
+A present command additionally requires one primary row, row 0 class 1, and exact
+command-frontier equality with the cohort. Its scheduler identity is a sequence.
+Initial and maximum raw icount each permit 0..UINT64_MAX, with maximum at least
+initial. Logical-tick and native-nanosecond ceilings each independently permit
+0..UINT64_MAX; no arithmetic or ordering across those distinct domains is
+inferred. Command frontier permits 0..UINT64_MAX and must equal the cohort's.
+Stop mode is exactly 1 or 2: mode 1 requires next-idle target 0; mode 2 requires a
+target generation. Reserved bytes 60..63 and 80..127 are always zero, including
+for a completed command: outcomes exist only in the receiver return record.
+An absent command has all 128 command bytes zero and row 0 cannot be class 1.
+These syntax rules cannot authenticate the required actual class 1 Original.
+
+Return sequence and original command sequence are sequences; loan and local
+serial are generations; ticket serial has the bound above. First and post are
+independently nonpositive i32, including INT32_MIN. Returned-view mask is a subset
+of 0xf and physical-child mask a subset of 0x7f; either may be zero as metadata.
+Outcome is 0 (none), 1 (ceiling) or 2 (next-idle), completed units is 0 or 1, and they
+are either both zero or both nonzero. Work-unit serial is zero exactly when
+units is zero, otherwise a generation. Next-idle completion is a generation
+exactly for outcome 2, otherwise zero. Final raw icount permits 0..UINT64_MAX;
+actual initial/ceiling comparisons belong to retained operation authority, not
+this standalone decoder. Negative first/post may coexist with an actual unit;
+the codec preserves that metadata without declaring successful completion.
+
+Terminal, original command and receiver return sequences are sequences; loan is
+a generation and ticket serial has the bound above. First/post are independently
+nonpositive i32. Born mask is nonzero and a subset of 0xf; consumed mask is a
+subset of born, including zero or incomplete consumption. Command-consumption
+generation permits zero (no observed consumption) or a generation; UINT64_MAX
+refuses. Partial consumption and negative post are preserved without claiming a
+Consumed transition. A real producer must independently prove every required
+physical return before that transition, and retain actual consumed facts when
+its independent post fails.
+
+The four C entry points first require nonnull input/output, an output aligned
+for its process-private view, exact input length, nonoverflowing complete input
+and output pointer extents, and disjoint spans. Refusal leaves output unchanged.
+Null, alignment and overlap refuse with EINVAL; length with EMSGSIZE; extent
+arithmetic overflow with EOVERFLOW; unsupported magic/schema/declared length
+with EPROTONOSUPPORT; other new syntax with EPROTO. Nested cohort errors retain
+the existing cohort decoder's status. These checks neither probe pointer backing
+nor replace the caller's obligation to supply actual valid stable byte storage.
+
+Ticket transition writers
+-------------------------
+
+The complete allowed ticket transition table is:
+
+=========================== ========== ========================================
+From to                     Writer     Required real custody
+=========================== ========== ========================================
+Unpublished zero to Issued   Producer   Fresh paid recipient-specific borrower.
+Closed to fresh Issued       Producer   Prior terminal consumed and local Closed;
+                                       fresh nonwrapping serial and real birth.
+Issued to Acquired          Receiver   Exact issued ticket after stable snapshot.
+Issued to Withdrawn         Producer   CAS wins against receiver acquisition.
+Withdrawn to fresh Issued    Producer   Actual withdrawn offer memberships and
+                                       command borrower destroyed and journaled;
+                                       fresh nonwrapping serial and real birth.
+Acquired to ReturnRequested Receiver   Work children and actual callbacks joined.
+ReturnRequested to Consumed Producer   Every producer membership/command returned.
+Consumed to Closed          Receiver   Independent post and own tail returns done.
+=========================== ========== ========================================
+
+Withdrawal does not itself free an offer. Its producer must complete and retain
+all destructor facts before reclamation. A failed/uncertain destructor keeps
+Withdrawn and its actual holders. A reader that has not acquired may still hold
+the session mapping, but holds no operation membership: its failed CAS and
+independent snapshot refusal release only its own real read borrow. The
+producer cannot unmap the session behind that reader. No ticket transition can
+restore permission, zero a live debt, skip a phase or repeat a completed release.
+
+Receiver acquisition and current
+--------------------------------
+
+The first receiver word at 1792 is a ticket: ``serial << 3 | phase``. Phases are
+Issued 1, Acquired 2, ReturnRequested 3, Consumed 4, Closed 5 and Withdrawn 6. Serial
+zero and phase 0/7 refuse. The producer writes Issued only while constructing a
+new actual prepaid recipient-specific borrower; this is a physical producer
+operation before publication, not an entitlement inferred from its word.
+Only after the prior command's real terminal return and Closed may it reuse the
+region with a fresh serial. Neither serial nor publication sequence may wrap.
+
+The receiver first retains the actual local member/runtime/VMA/slot/lender
+references and canonical holder under its genuine payer. It takes a full stable
+publication snapshot, then compare-exchanges the exact issued ticket to
+Acquired and independently reobserves sequence, ticket, permission and the same
+local Source/Main/module identities. The successful CAS consumes a producer's
+already physically retained, specifically issued borrower. Decoded fields and
+CAS success alone are never a Source Original or class 1 issuer. Failure after
+CAS retains the acquired debt and proceeds to the same authenticated return.
+
+Producer withdrawal races only through CAS Issued to Withdrawn. A failed
+withdrawal against Acquired cannot free memberships. Revoked permission refuses
+new effects but cannot discharge Acquired or ReturnRequested. A receiver stalled
+before CAS cannot acquire a withdrawn or replaced ticket. Its final sequence
+observation rejects a changed snapshot; it still holds the actual VMA lifetime.
+
+Current uses the retained immutable command, the actual private Original/holder,
+nonwrapping local serial and real CPU child. It independently checks the same
+publication sequence, exact Acquired ticket and Borrowable phase before and
+after effects, plus genuine Original time/cancellation constraints. It does not
+copy all rows per instruction, request a socket reply or count observation as
+progress. Producer policy changes that cannot preserve this immutable contract
+must revoke permission; they may not overwrite a held command.
+
+Receiver return span
+--------------------
+
+Receiver offset 1800 begins its return record (504 remaining bytes of the 512-byte
+span). It has BEu64 return publication sequence at 0, exact ticket serial at 8,
+offer/loan generation at 16, original command publication sequence at 24,
+receiver nonwrapping local serial at 32, BEi32 first/post at 40/44, BEu32 locally
+returned row-view mask at 48 and physical-child-return mask at 52, BEu32 outcome
+kind at 56 and completed-unit count at 60, BEu64 final raw icount at 64, authenticated
+next-idle completion generation at 72, work-unit publication serial at 80 and
+zero 88..503. Physical-child bits are module semantic guard/callback 0,
+material/worker leaves 1, effect borrows 2, RR execution/cleanup 3, CPU/QOM children 4,
+next-idle subscription/callback 5 and work-unit publication return 6. Each bit
+reports the corresponding actual owner return; an absent constructor cannot
+be filled with an all-returned constant. Class-specific required masks must
+come from the actual operation's independently established child inventory,
+not from these decoded bits. No mask or status is itself an
+owner.
+
+Before ReturnRequested, the receiver joins the actual module semantic guard
+and its callback return; each per-operation material/worker leaf; register or
+other effect borrow; actual RR execution-end and longjmp cleanup completion;
+all CPU/QOM execution children; any next-idle subscription/callback; and actual
+work-unit publication return. These are the operation's work children, not the
+session service needed to publish their terminal record. Unimplemented child
+constructors or unknown return masks keep the operation unavailable.
+
+Separate paid session/cleanup holders retain the region, doorbells, setup slot,
+module code, parser and nominal member/runtime through final post and last
+unrefs. They are not row memberships required to return before their own
+service can report consumption. The producer must likewise retain an independent
+service/cleanup owner after consuming the operation memberships. A transport
+tail cannot be secretly paid by the very Original whose physical return it
+would await. The current real module/runtime registration can supply lifetime
+pins only where its genuine owner closes this distinction; otherwise the new
+capability stays unavailable. No protocol mask declares these holders paid.
+
+The receiver closes new borrowers, joins those actual work children, saves
+first/post and serializes this exact terminal evidence, then publishes an even return sequence and CASes its exact Acquired ticket to
+ReturnRequested. The real work-unit producer must have published exactly one
+unit for healthy completion, after either actual retired ceiling/icount or the
+same command's authenticated next-idle completion. Instruction/loop/callback/ACK
+observations cannot supply this witness. Failure can return physical custody
+without successful semantic work; post failure does not erase a real completed
+unit. Terminal serialization reports actual witnesses retained by those owners.
+
+Doorbells notify the existing poll/idle path after publication; they grant no
+permission, progress, unit or physical return. Polling the shared atomic state
+is sufficient for correctness. Lost/coalesced/spurious doorbells cannot change
+ownership. Both actual kernel event roles and dispatch lifetimes remain paid.
+
+Producer terminal span and final closure
+---------------------------------------
+
+Terminal offset 2304 contains BEu64 terminal publication sequence at 0, exact
+ticket serial at 8, offer/loan generation at 16, original command sequence at 24,
+receiver return sequence at 32, BEi32 first/post at 40/44, BEu32 actually consumed
+membership mask at 48 and original born mask at 52, BEu64 actual immutable-command
+borrower consumption generation at 56, and zero 64..511. A producer takes its own
+stable receiver-return snapshot and physically returns each actual cohort
+membership and command borrower. It journals each completed destructor before
+any fallible post; unknown results keep real debt and actual holders.
+
+Only after all born memberships and the command borrower physically return may
+the producer publish that terminal record, independently post it, and transition
+the exact ReturnRequested ticket to Consumed. This physical transition must
+still be published when post is negative: retain the first/post statuses and
+actual consumed mask in the terminal journal. Failure does not resurrect
+already consumed memberships. An incomplete/uncertain destructor cannot publish
+Consumed. Repeated receiver observation or notification reads the same terminal
+journal; no path invokes a second release. Producer terminal consumption is a
+real destructor edge, not acknowledgement of a parsed packet or changed scalar.
+
+Receiver independently validates the same terminal sequence, ticket, original
+publication and return sequence around its own post and actual exchange/parser
+borrow return. It retains separate producer-consumed and local-physically-closed
+facts. Its transport/module last-unref facts live in external Source journals,
+so no post reads a released module/payload. Callbacks and final unrefs remain
+outside the Source registry mutex. Only after those actual local returns may it
+CAS Consumed to Closed, clear its canonical holder and return applicable credit.
+Producer-consumed debt and receiver-Closed debt are distinct. There is no
+unbounded wait for either: busy or incomplete closure returns a typed refusal
+while preserving the same journal/holder for a later authenticated cleanup edge.
+The retained session service can observe or finish either side without entering
+a new Quantum. It cannot pretend the missing asynchronous Main/Core child exists.
+The lifetime mapping/doorbell owner remains retained for the session; its later
+unmap/close is not claimed by a per-command record.
+
+=================== ==========================================================
+Receiver state      Required physical transition
+=================== ==========================================================
+Unavailable         No real matched capability, issuer or payer exists.
+Snapshot            Retain actual mapping/member and stable offered bytes.
+Acquired            Consume real issued borrower; retain through failed post.
+Returning           Close new borrowers; join every actual local child return.
+ReturnRequested     Terminal evidence published; producer still retains debt.
+ConsumedObserved    Actual producer destructor journal matches exact loan.
+Closed              Independent post and all local physical returns complete.
+=================== ==========================================================
+
+Concrete implementation joins
+-----------------------------
+
+The producer counterpart must implement Setup 6/purpose-plan payment and retained
+roles, Hello 9 capability, actual ticket/cohort/immutable-command birth, CAS
+withdrawal/revocation and physical terminal destruction before availability.
+The generated public boundary package must define this region and exact checked
+geometry. This checkout must then add actual preexec lender construction and
+layout pricing, the matched module/member lifetime, process-private canonical
+publisher journal, mapped atomic entry/current/return and authenticated terminal
+closure. The four missing publisher-action bodies can call only that chain.
+
+Initial receiver work can implement canonical geometry/atomic snapshots and
+retained negative/uncertain journals behind unavailable capability. It cannot
+issue a publisher from a valid packet, static capacity, standing schema promise,
+existing scalar callback or arbitrary mapping. The actual class 1 module/Core
+Original, paid runtime/handler/allocator, RR/CPU/QOM child and genuine outcome
+producers remain separate prerequisites. Existing selected SDK/Quantum admission
+and request-return refusal stay unchanged until all genuine joins exist.
+
+
+Implemented codec scope
+-----------------------
+
+``crucible-publisher-protocol.h`` supplies process-private decoded views only.
+The four actual codec bodies in ``crucible-process-purpose.c`` validate stable
+caller-owned copies of the exact header/publication/return/terminal spans.
+They check canonical offsets, bounds, reserved bytes, supported syntax, complete
+cohort decoding, distinct domains, nonwrapping identities and signed statuses,
+including consumed metadata with a negative post. Full input/output pointer
+extents, output alignment and disjointness are checked before mutation. Every
+refusal preserves the output. They do not access a mapping, run atomic snapshot
+or CAS, retain a physical object or implement any ownership-state transition.
+The public format remains a proposal until the paired boundary implementation
+and registry integration; these codecs advertise no capability.
+
+Independent C vectors provide literal big-endian bytes without a production
+encoder or accepting owner fixture. Valid byte syntax is factual only. Tests
+include zero/max ceilings, both stop modes, complete and partially projected
+four-row cohorts, incomplete and complete consumed masks with negative post,
+unit/next-idle shapes, reserved spans, canonical geometry, short input, pointer
+overflow, output overlap and unchanged refusal outputs. Existing process-purpose
+controls and authenticated preinit extraction remain separate preserved checks.
