 executed instructions can then be used to calculate QEMU_CLOCK_VIRTUAL
 which represents the amount of elapsed time in the system since
 execution started. Depending on the icount mode this may either be a
-fixed number of ns per instruction or adjusted as execution continues
+fixed number of ps per instruction or adjusted as execution continues
 to keep wall clock time and virtual time in sync.
 
+For ordinary TCG, ``-icount shift=N`` assigns ``2^N`` nanoseconds to
+each retired instruction. Thus ``shift=0`` is 1 ns per instruction,
+while ``shift=10`` is 1,024 ns per instruction. The internal virtual
+clock and its timer deadlines use picoseconds, preserving fractional
+nanosecond deadlines; public nanosecond clock APIs project that
+coordinate down to nanoseconds.
+The Crucible ``sim`` accelerator uses its separate fixed model of
+50 ps per retired instruction and requires ``shift=0``.
